# Git: say commit/push status at the end

The user cannot easily tell whether the agent already committed and pushed.
Never leave that implicit or buried in the middle of a long reply.

## Required closing line

End **every** user-facing reply that involved repo work (code, docs, rules,
tests, metadata) with a short, plain **Git:** line. Use one of:

- `Git: not committed or pushed.` — changes exist only in the working tree
  (or there were no git-relevant changes).
- `Git: committed on \`branch\` (\`hash\`), not pushed.` — rare; avoid; push
  after commit per `git-push-after-commit.mdc`.
- `Git: committed and pushed to \`branch\` (\`hash\`).` — feature branch on
  `origin`.
- `Git: committed, merged to \`main\`, pushed (\`hash\`). Feature branch
  deleted.` — Coolify-ready path.
- `Git: no repo changes.` — investigation-only or chat-only turn.

Include the short commit hash when a commit exists. If a Coolify deploy is
still needed after a `main` push, say that in the same closing block.

## Do not

- Soften or hide the status (“should be on main”, “ready when you want”).
- Skip the line because the reply is short or the user did not ask about Git.
- Commit or push silently without that closing status.
